{"id": "bdb5b70b-7b62-41d8-a897-23b4d4d301ca", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T12:16:30.862220", "source": "user", "parent_id": "e85a4217-0859-4d48-95c6-528f9bc1d1d1", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "Before we commit to a design, please do a scoping pass only — no full solution yet.\n\n1) Input/artifact inspection: report what actually exists in /workspace (any dataset files, RepoBench/CrossFileFirst splits, model weights, harness or eval scripts, cached completion files, config files), plus the usable runtime facts (Python version, installed packages relevant to code completion eval, CPU/GPU availability, network status). If RepoBench Python 'cross_file_first' data or model weights are absent and cannot be obtained offline, say so explicitly rather than assuming they are present.\n\n2) Proposed approach outline (high level, not the full design): how you would operationalize the comparison for two models of very different scale on this specific setting — which model checkpoints/configurations you would treat as \"GPT-2\" and \"CodeLlama-7b\", what the 'cross_file_first' unit of evaluation is (one completion per target file from the first cross-file dependency, per the RepoBench convention), what decoding setup you would standardize, and which headline metrics you would report (e.g., exact match, edit similarity, codebleu-style measures) with what sample sizes / stratification.\n\n3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties you want me to rule on, stated as questions, for example: whether we hold the tokenizer/context budget constant or let each model use its native context window; whether we compare at fixed parameter count (impossible here) or treat the comparison as a \"general small vs code-specialized mid-size\" contrast and therefore must report scale-matched caveats; whether compute-matched (per-token or per-FLOP) reporting is required given the ~100x parameter gap; whether instruction/chat variants and precision/quantization are in scope; and whether any retrieval/dependency-selection component of cross-file prompt construction is part of the comparison or fixed by using the official pre-built prompts.\n\nKeep it to inspection findings, the skeleton approach, and those open decisions. Do not run evaluations or produce the final design in this turn.", "required_handoff_stage": "plan", "approach_approved": false, "public_task": {"question": "How does the performance of a small language model (e.g., GPT-2) compare to a larger code-specialized model (e.g., CodeLlama-7b) on the RepoBench Python dataset for the 'cross_file_first' setting?", "context": "Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.", "environment": {"runtime": "bubblewrap", "network": "disabled", "repository": "/workspace", "python": "python3", "software_environment": "system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled", "datasets": [], "gpu_enabled": false, "cpu_affinity_count": 224, "note": "Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment."}, "researcher_topics": [], "question_budget": 6, "actor_step_budget": 60, "continue_after_initial_plan": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
